      "id": 1,
      "text": "Alamo’s name comes from the Spanish word álamo, meaning poplar, and was chosen to honor the Alamo Mission in San Antonio.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alamo%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After Mission San José was founded in 1797, its grazing area stretched across the entire San Ramon Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alamo%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first permanent post office in the area opened on August 20, 1852, with John Jones as its inaugural postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Alamo,_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Tatcan, a Bay Miwok tribe, inhabited the Alamo area and managed San Ramon Creek’s watershed with controlled burns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
